# /etc/litestream/env -- root:root, mode 0600. NOT IN GIT, ever.
#
#   install -d -m 0700 -o root -g root /etc/litestream
#   install -m 0600 -o root -g root env.example /etc/litestream/env
#   ${EDITOR:-vi} /etc/litestream/env
#
# systemd loads this into litestream's process environment (EnvironmentFile= in
# litestream.service). /etc/litestream.yml then refers to these by name only, so
# the generated config is safe to read, diff and paste into a ticket.
#
# THE NAMES BELOW ARE THE CANONICAL SET. Litestream 0.3.13 reads exactly one
# environment variable on its own (LITESTREAM_CONFIG); every other name here
# matters only because the generated config spells it inside ${...}. So the
# names are ours to choose, and these -- LITESTREAM_R2_* -- are the choice.
# If an older box has LITESTREAM_ACCESS_KEY_ID / LITESTREAM_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY /
# R2_ENDPOINT / R2_BUCKET, rename them; on 0.3.13 those spellings are read by
# nothing at all.
#
# litestream-sync-config.sh REFUSES TO RUN if this file is missing, is not owned
# by root, is readable by group or other, leaves any of the five values below
# empty, or spells the endpoint with a URL scheme. An empty key/secret is worth
# the paranoia specifically: litestream hands an empty pair to the AWS default
# credential chain instead, so it would try ~/.aws and the instance metadata
# service and fail as something that looks nothing like "you typo'd a variable".
#
# Format is systemd's EnvironmentFile, not shell: no `export`, no command
# substitution, no quotes unless the value genuinely contains them.

# The R2 S3 endpoint for YOUR Cloudflare account. Dashboard -> R2 -> your bucket
# -> Settings -> "S3 API"; the account id is the 32 hex characters in front.
#
# BARE HOST, NO SCHEME. This is the one canonical spelling and the generator
# rejects anything else. Litestream itself would accept either (aws-sdk-go
# prepends https:// to a scheme-less custom endpoint), but the runbooks compose
# "https://${LITESTREAM_R2_ENDPOINT}" for aws-cli and rclone, and
# "https://https://..." is not a thing you want to be reading at 3am.
LITESTREAM_R2_ENDPOINT=CHANGEME0123456789abcdef0123456789.r2.cloudflarestorage.com

# The bucket. One bucket for the whole fleet; tenants are prefixes inside it
# (tenants/<label>/store.db), so there is nothing to create per signup.
LITESTREAM_R2_BUCKET=passband-tenant-backups

# An R2 API token scoped to THAT BUCKET with Object Read & Write. Not an account
# token, and not Admin: this credential lives on an internet-facing box, and the
# blast radius of it leaking should be "this one bucket", not "all of R2".
LITESTREAM_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=CHANGEME_r2_access_key_id
LITESTREAM_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=CHANGEME_r2_secret_access_key

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLIENT-SIDE ENCRYPTION. THE PUBLIC HALF, AND ONLY THE PUBLIC HALF.
#
# The age recipient for the backup keypair. Litestream 0.3.x seals every
# snapshot and every WAL segment to this before it uploads, so what lands in R2
# is age ciphertext and Cloudflare cannot read a tenant's mail. (0.5.x removed
# age support entirely; that is why this fleet is pinned to 0.3.13.)
#
# Generate the pair ONCE, on the box, and read this value out of the identity
# file's header comment:
#
#   umask 077
#   age-keygen -o /etc/litestream/backup-age.key     # prints the public key
#   age-keygen -y /etc/litestream/backup-age.key     # or re-derive it later
#
# A recipient is a public key: it is not secret, and it is not enough to read a
# backup. An EMPTY one is not silently plaintext -- litestream 0.3.13 refuses to
# start with "no recipients found" -- but the generator checks it here anyway so
# the failure is a sentence about a variable and not a 3am outage.
LITESTREAM_AGE_RECIPIENT=age1CHANGEMExxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE IDENTITY (PRIVATE KEY) IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THIS FILE.
#
# Replication needs only the recipient, so the litestream process that runs
# around the clock cannot open a single backup it writes. Restoring needs the
# identity, and the restoring operator exports it by hand, for the minutes a
# restore takes:
#
#   export LITESTREAM_AGE_IDENTITY="$(grep -v '^#' /etc/litestream/backup-age.key | tr -d '[:space:]')"
#
# >>> LOSE THE IDENTITY AND EVERY BACKUP IN R2 IS PERMANENTLY UNREADABLE. <<<
# >>> Not by us, not by Cloudflare, not by anyone. There is no escrow, and  <<<
# >>> a key that exists only on the box it protects is not a backup key.    <<<
# >>>                                                                       <<<
# >>> PUT /etc/litestream/backup-age.key IN THE PASSWORD MANAGER THE DAY    <<<
# >>> YOU GENERATE IT, before you generate a single byte of backup, and     <<<
# >>> store it beside the tenant identity Secrets dump -- the two are the   <<<
# >>> only things on this box that no amount of re-syncing can rebuild.     <<<
#
# On disk it is root:root 0600 and nothing reads it automatically. If it is
# EVER exposed, the recovery is: mint a new pair, change LITESTREAM_AGE_RECIPIENT
# here, restart litestream, and keep the old identity forever -- backups written
# before the swap are still sealed to the old key.
